<title>UK: Brit Energy says no windfall tax provision.</title>
<headline>Brit Energy says no windfall tax provision.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-06-04</dateline>
<text>
<p>Britain's nuclear power generator British Energy Plc said on Wednesday it did not think it should be subject to a &quot;windfall&quot; tax and had made no provision for it.</p>
<p>&quot;This is the first profit we've made,&quot; said chief executive Robert Hawley in an interview.</p>
<p>The company earlier reported a 61 million stg profit for the year to March 31 compared with a pro forma loss of 155 million the year before. The generator was privatised last year.</p>
<p>&quot;We're only nine months old. You can hardly say that our shareholders have had a great return,&quot; he said.</p>
<p>As the company had been making losses up until now Hawley said it should be subject to a rebate rather than a tax.</p>
<p>The new Labour government has proposed a one-off windfall tax to fund a three billion stg plan to cut unemployment.</p>
